{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for design head365f56a5f1c4d2deea4d1669f7f26c5eb3bff331, tree4861b79798b5a7116121313330afc1ee74417bd8. Read primary7d940dbb first and in full (674 lines, including the separately retrieved middle), the complete added Order paragraph1763634e, the referenced native seed/creation, entry/use, delivery/effect/replay forms, archive15.1–15.8 and Plan033 ordering. These findings concern the proposed protocol, not a runtime implementation.\n\nF1 — P1: a returned cut can change after a lost allocation reply. Primary lines346–370 and399–418. Allocate writes pending only after receiving the order scope's answer, while at(P) knows unfinished work only from that local row. Counterexample: order commits allocation1 to A but its reply is lost; A has no pending row. B receives2, reads A at2 and commits using A's genesis. A retries, receives its idempotent1 and commits there. A's last positioned entry below2 is now different, so B's earlier read no longer equals the specified cut and its replay fails. A live allocation with no member entry is indistinguishable from the claimed harmless hole. Clearing a refused allocation without a durable terminal fence also permits the same input to be retried at that old position after another reader proceeded. Define a durable resolution/finality rule that readers can prove for every relevant earlier allocation, including unknown replies, cancellation/refusal and late retries; a completed hole must never later gain a commit. Preserve monotonic member positions and immutable cuts, without treating absence as completion. The logical trace in /private/tmp/checker-order-scope-ebx8dtr6/review-validation.json executes the literal cut rule and demonstrates the changed answer; it is not a native runtime test.\n\nF2 — P1: the restart rule cannot provide its stated finite progress. Lines315–335,351–378 and498–501. The only persisted pending data are input digest and P. They contain neither the original input nor a deadline, so an evicted object cannot recover an intent's notAfter or judge that original by itself. A sequenced delivery has a DeliveryCause, not a signed intent lifetime at all. Nothing states what alarm/read resumes or expires it when its sender does not return, or how later inputs are handled while the one row remains. A stopped member can therefore block readers indefinitely; the stated one-input-lifetime limit does not follow. Specify the retained original/proof and reservation, all relevant deadlines, autonomous wake/recovery, new-input admission, and delivery-specific completion. Coordinate this with F1's terminal fence; do not silently abandon an already admitted delivery duty to manufacture a timeout.\n\nF3 — P1: the read witness has two incompatible byte domains and does not yet define replayable absence. Lines283–288 say uses retains the cut entry and its content digest;416–418 instead put the read page's digest there. Current FactUse.content is the digest of canonical foreign entry bytes (packages/contract/src/entry.ts:105; packages/replay/src/verify.ts:1475–1485), fetched as retained kind entry and decoded as an Entry. A page is not that entry. Define the exact typed witness and domains for read name/range, full member identity, P, cut/closed-prefix proof and canonical page, with retention and archive consumers and finite byte bounds. Specify the left-member empty-page case too:5.7 promises an empty page after the leave fence, while5.8 unconditionally requires a cut entry and the member journal for each read. Replay must distinguish verified absence, unresolved allocation and missing dependency and reconstruct the exact page without current bookkeeping or live state. No backward compatibility is required, so the forms may change; the new forms must be stated consistently.\n\nF4 — P2: group creation does not yet bind the promised identities. Lines243–251,300–307 and611. The order genesis fixes member seed IDs and also opens member rows with fixed scope references before the member incarnations exist; each member genesis requires the order's full scope reference. Native ScopeRef includes a random genesis incarnation, and Seed.cause is derived from the actual creating input (contract/scope.ts:44–52,62; derive/genesis.ts:81–95). Creating members from the order's result changes that cause; saying IDs are known from seeds alone does not give the promised construction. State one complete bootstrap/confirmation sequence, including any new seed/planned-creation form, when each full incarnation becomes bound, partial creation failures and refusal of another incarnation/cross-room membership. This is a missing construction, not a claim that fields themselves participate in the seed hash.\n\nF5 — P2: section2's stated test contradicts archive admission. Lines81–99 say bindings only change copying and no judge reads them, but195–201 makes install read the binding and change its answer to archive-unavailable. The immutable directory definition also gains an act/slot for that mutable value. Keep Hugh's archive-as-binding decision, but distinguish execution-level semantics, immutable declared host requirements and mutable room policy values; do not justify it by the false claim that judgment never reads the binding. Apply that revised distinction consistently to the table and section4.\n\nF6 — P2: late binding must include genesis and update the archive read contract. Lines186–207 say archive from position1 and preserve15.3–15.8 unchanged. Native genesis is seq0 (contract/entry.ts:17); omitting it breaks clone identity verification and replay, which15.3/15.8 promise. Require the full prefix beginning at0, including a genesis-only scope and its definition closure. Also list the required prospective15.6 wording change: it currently says Recorded scopes have no archive member and no archive member means the level is not Archived. Those statements cannot remain unchanged once bound Recorded/Sequenced scopes archive. Preserve receipt-at-own-position, through/read-back meaning, detached-text omission, conflict/lost-reply rules and existing finite writer limits.\n\nF7 — P2: the lag paragraph conflates guard refusal with conditional effects. Lines533–540 say an old delivery runs the unless branch and may be refused. A handler-level guard failure currently refuses it without executing the handler's effects; if/unless are conditions on individual effects (docs/scopes.md, Effects). State explicitly whether lag is allowed in those conditions as well as top-level guards, and give the two separate behaviors: top-level refusal with no effects, or an accepted handler whose conditional alternatives record the miss. Do not imply both occur for one refused handler. Include replay and clock-behind behavior for the chosen forms.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: changes required, chiefly F1–F4. The distinction between one owner and optional ordered reads is useful, and no cross-scope atomic update is claimed. Cuts need durable finality, not just a pending row. O4's proposed explanation also needs correction: a local unsequenced update after the last sequenced entry makes the live head different from the cut defined in5.5; explicitly say which state own-subject guards read.\n\nSecurity conclusion: changes required for incarnation-bound group admission and verifiable read/finality evidence. The archive binding remains subject to its existing credential/policy/disclosure boundaries; this review grants no archive enablement, room destruction or deployment.\n\nSimplification conclusion: retain one explicit allocation/resolution protocol and one typed read witness rather than relying on unjournaled exceptions. The current Order paragraph otherwise accurately describes today's unordered delivery/relate behavior. Slices are prospectively consistent with Plan033's minimum order: after M1/G0/M2a, S2 before demo-room archive slice9, and no displacement of G5a beforeG5 or archive enablement beforeG4. Document repairs at S2/S4 should include F6's affected read contract.\n\nValidation: exact two-path delta from common reviewed Mainfecc;2,118 candidate leaves with2,116 prior leaves unchanged,14 local Markdown link targets resolve, whitespace passes. The later Main9c32 G0 work is separate and must be preserved at any future landing. Both exact candidate artifact bindings are fresh; the path query is exhausted. Bibliographic titles/authors were checked at the primary Berkeley report and https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.02444; the protocol counterexamples above are deductions from this note and repository contracts, not claims proved by those papers. No package installation, source/ref/provider mutation, runtime gate or unchanged suite repeat. Request29e9024d/3e4a remains open for a corrected exact head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7d940dbbd700757a784b3dcd115b49c65cf20ce6","binding":"assigned","head":"365f56a5f1c4d2deea4d1669f7f26c5eb3bff331","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3a61421789a0f9e78e1df6dafa3a04095824b37b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3e4a926151f6bdb386dade53b343c35eef2496dd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e077050793edd3071e988f12548e236b80840983","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}